      "id": "E08",
      "chapter": 8,
      "slug": "08-infinite-experiments",
      "section": "8.3",
      "lang": "en",
      "title": "Three valid pairs need not share one world",
      "html": "<figure class=\"explain-figure early-figure\" id=\"visual-E08\" data-early=\"E08\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>E08</span><h3>Three valid pairs need not share one world</h3></header><p class=\"\">Each opposite pair can fairly take 01 or 10. But no triple can make all three pairs opposite.</p><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-pairs=\"impossible\" aria-pressed=\"true\">All opposite</button><button type=\"button\" data-pairs=\"consistent\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Third pair equal</button></div><div class=\"early-single\"><img class=\"ex-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v1/E08-en-impossible-0.svg\" alt=\"A binary triangle violates one of three opposite constraints\" data-pair-scene></div><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-flip=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"true\">010</button><button type=\"button\" data-flip=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">101</button></div><output data-reading>The first two relations hold; the third must fail.</output><figcaption id=\"visual-E08-end\">Each Xi is a fair bit. An opposite pair has law 01,10 with probability 1/2 each; an equal pair has law 00,11 with probability 1/2 each. If all three pairs were opposite almost surely, X1≠X2 and X2≠X3 would force X1=X3, a contradiction. This is not inferred merely from one displayed assignment. With the third pair equal, the joint law P(010)=P(101)=1/2 realizes all three pair laws. Buttons flip all bits, retaining the same fair one-dimensional marginals. The picture shows a necessary consistency condition, not the extension theorem.</figcaption><nav class=\"ex-downloads\"><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v1/E08.en.svg\">Complete static SVG</a><a href=\"/print/E08.en.html\">Print view</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "Each Xi is a fair bit. An opposite pair has law 01,10 with probability 1/2 each; an equal pair has law 00,11 with probability 1/2 each. If all three pairs were opposite almost surely, X1≠X2 and X2≠X3 would force X1=X3, a contradiction. This is not inferred merely from one displayed assignment. With the third pair equal, the joint law P(010)=P(101)=1/2 realizes all three pair laws. Buttons flip all bits, retaining the same fair one-dimensional marginals. The picture shows a necessary consistency condition, not the extension theorem.",
      "status": "author visual review pending"
    },
