      "id": "E16",
      "chapter": 16,
      "slug": "16-stopping-times",
      "section": "16.1",
      "lang": "en",
      "title": "Can the same present justify the decision?",
      "html": "<figure class=\"explain-figure early-figure\" id=\"visual-E16\" data-early=\"E16\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>E16</span><h3>Can the same present justify the decision?</h3></header><p class=\"\">After 0→1→0, the first return is known. Whether it is the last still depends on unseen steps.</p><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-reveal=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"true\">See only the present</button><button type=\"button\" data-reveal=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Reveal two futures</button></div><div class=\"early-single\"><img class=\"ex-svg\" src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v1/E16-en-0.svg\" alt=\"A shared random-walk prefix with its future hidden\" data-prefix-scene></div><output data-reading>T=2 is decidable now; L=2 is not.</output><figcaption id=\"visual-E16-end\">Simple symmetric walk starts at 0 with its natural filtration. T=inf{n≥1:Sn=0}; L=max{0≤n≤4:Sn=0}. The shared prefix (0,1,0) is one F2 atom. The positive-probability continuations (1,2) and (1,0) give L=2 and L=4 respectively, while both give T=2. Hence {L≤2} is not constant on that atom and is not an F2 event. Two completions suffice to disprove decidability; they do not enumerate all futures or receive an arbitrary total probability. A legitimate stopping rule alone does not justify unbounded optional sampling; its UI conditions remain in the text.</figcaption><nav class=\"ex-downloads\"><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-early-v1/E16.en.svg\">Complete static SVG</a><a href=\"/print/E16.en.html\">Print view</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "Simple symmetric walk starts at 0 with its natural filtration. T=inf{n≥1:Sn=0}; L=max{0≤n≤4:Sn=0}. The shared prefix (0,1,0) is one F2 atom. The positive-probability continuations (1,2) and (1,0) give L=2 and L=4 respectively, while both give T=2. Hence {L≤2} is not constant on that atom and is not an F2 event. Two completions suffice to disprove decidability; they do not enumerate all futures or receive an arbitrary total probability. A legitimate stopping rule alone does not justify unbounded optional sampling; its UI conditions remain in the text.",
      "status": "author visual review pending"
    },
